# Airport COO Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Chair speaking:** Chief Operating Officer, top-tier US hub. Twenty-five years in airport operations, including time on the airline side. Reports to the CEO. Owns airfield ops, terminal ops, facilities and maintenance, engineering, environmental, planning, and airline relations. The OPSCOMM is in my pocket. I know which gate has the broken jet bridge before the inbound captain does.

**Bottom line up front:** The thesis is directionally right — a common MWAA acoustic and information-integrity standard belongs in the Design Manual, and it belongs there before Concourse E's next-generation siblings enter schematic design. But "quiet" is the wrong frame from the ops chair. The frame is **information integrity under stress.** On a bluebird Tuesday at DCA, silence sells. On a January thunderstorm at IAD with three rolling ground stops in the Baltimore-Washington triangle, a fielded passenger-boarding-bridge at C-27, forty rebook lines, and an unaccompanied minor missing a Munich connection, the airport PA is the only channel I control that reaches every square foot of my building in under fifteen seconds. Any standard MWAA writes has to specify both modes and specify who owns the switch between them.

---

## 1. Key findings

1. **The operator controls maybe 40 percent of the noise floor in a US hub terminal.** Airport-owned PA and background music sit on top of airline gate-agent scripts, TSA queue announcements, CBP arrival messaging, concessions televisions, jet-bridge alarms, moving-walkway end-warnings, cleaning equipment, and thirty or forty tenant systems each with their own broadcast rights inside their leased envelope. Any standard that does not name each channel, name the party who controls it, and specify how the standard binds that party, is decorative.
2. **NFPA 72 is the floor, and the floor is 0.50 STI average and 0.45 minimum in every Acoustically Distinguishable Space.** Voice evacuation intelligibility is a life-safety requirement, not a passenger-experience preference. A quiet-by-design standard that degrades emergency intelligibility gets red-lined by the AHJ before it leaves design review. The MWAA spec must specify the STI envelope in emergency mode and prove it under IEC 60268-16 STIPA methodology at commissioning [Source: https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/26771].
3. **The single largest predictor of a missed connection at a big US hub is a gate change inside T-25 minutes.** In that window, FIDS updates lag actual gate assignment by 60–120 seconds, the airline app is inconsistent (it works for the frequent flyer with notifications enabled and fails for the eight passengers who need it most), and the human at the gate is juggling four things. A silence-first standard punishes this exact use case if it is not surgical.
4. **The operator gets the building handed back broken on PA and audio work in three predictable places:** speaker coverage overlap in transition zones, adjacent-zone spillover into hold rooms, and PBB-alarm acoustics coupling into the boarding call. Every one of these is a design-review-stage catch that ops does not get invited to and cannot enforce after certificate of occupancy. A Design Manual standard is the only lever that puts the operator in the room before the acoustic model is signed.
5. **DCA and IAD are the same authority and completely different buildings.** DCA Terminal 1 is a 1941 Art Deco headhouse with hard-plaster surfaces and nine gates; DCA Terminal 2 is a 1997 Pelli hall; the two are not connected post-security. IAD is a Saarinen icon fronting midfield concourses reached today by mobile lounges and AeroTrain [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md]. A single prescriptive number will fail one of these buildings. A tiered standard — mandatory acoustic performance outcomes, context-dependent implementation — is the only one that operationalizes.
6. **Employee exposure is a live liability that never appears in the strategy deck.** OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95 requires a hearing conservation program at 85 dBA time-weighted average and engineering controls at 90 dBA [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1910/subpart-G/section-1910.95]. Gate agents in a peak-hour hold room during a de-ice cascade — with rolling airport PA, gate-agent handhelds on speaker, PBB alarms, and airline boarding-call chatter — are in that exposure zone. Nobody puts that in the capital ROI model; it sits on the workers' comp ledger.
7. **The 2026 window is the right window and it is closing.** Concourse E (435,000 sq ft, 14 United gates, direct AeroTrain access, 46,000 sq ft of concessions, 40,000-sq-ft United Club) opens later in 2026 [Source: https://thepointsguy.com/news/united-dream-terminal-dulles/]. The July 2026 joint MWAA/United/USDOT announcement puts more than 5 million square feet of IAD terminal space into design over the coming decade [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md]. DCA is not getting a rebuild but is getting roughly $2.4 billion of gate, curb, and terminal capital under the 2025 Use & Lease. If the acoustic and PA standard is not in the Design Manual by early 2027, it will be too late for the first tranche of that capital, and every subsequent project will inherit whatever gets built by default in Concourse E.
8. **The 2025 Use & Lease is the political constraint.** The two-airport residual model with a 140 percent debt-service coverage target makes the signatory airlines co-signers on any capital that touches rate base [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md]. A quiet standard framed as passenger experience will draw majority-in-interest objections. A standard framed as life-safety intelligibility, employee-exposure compliance, and reliability under IROPS is defensible in the airline consultation. The framing decision is not cosmetic.

