## 2. Evidence base

### Regulatory floor the standard cannot override

- **NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code):** Voice evacuation systems must achieve a Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space, with an average of at least 0.50 STI [Source: NFPA 72-2022, Chapter 24 Emergency Communications Systems and Annex D Speech Intelligibility]. Enforced by the AHJ at commissioning. Not negotiable.
- **IEC 60268-16:2020** defines the STI model and the STIPA field-measurement method [Source: https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/26771]. This is the instrumentation and methodology the acoustic commissioning agent will use. Specify it in the standard, or accept whatever the vendor uses.
- **OSHA 29 CFR 1910.95** requires a hearing conservation program at 85 dBA TWA and engineering or administrative controls at the 90 dBA permissible exposure limit [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1910/subpart-G/section-1910.95].
- **FAA AC 150/5360-12F, Airport Signing and Graphics** governs the visual-first information channel any quiet standard would substitute for audio [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentlibrary/media/advisory_circular/150_5360_12f.pdf].
- **ADA / Section 504 / ACAA:** accessible communication is a federal obligation. The redundant visual and digital channel for each announcement category has to be named in the standard; if not, the standard fails accessibility review.

### Research base

- **ACRP Report 52, Wayfinding and Signing Guidelines for Airport Terminals and Landside** — the industry base document for visual-first information design [Source: https://www.nationalacademies.org/publications/13640].
- **ACRP Report 161, Guidelines for Improving Airport Services for International Customers** — documents that international-passenger information effectiveness requires consistent verbal + visual + virtual redundancy [Source: https://www.greshamsmith.com/projects/acrp-report-161-guidelines-for-improving-airport-services-for-international-customers/]. This is direct evidence against a pure-quiet architecture at IAD, where 10.5 million international passengers moved through the building in 2025.
- **ACRP Report 177, Enhancing Airport Wayfinding for Aging Travelers and Persons with Disabilities** — accessibility-specific base document; eliminating audio disadvantages aging travelers, cognitive-difference travelers, and travelers with limited English proficiency [Source: https://www.greshamsmith.com/projects/acrp-report-177-enhancing-airport-wayfinding-for-aging-travelers-and-persons-with-disabilities/].
- **ACRP Report 25, Airport Passenger Terminal Planning and Design** — the base terminal planning reference [Source: https://onlinepubs.trb.org/onlinepubs/acrp/acrp_rpt_025v1.pdf].

### International "quiet" precedent, honestly read

- **Helsinki-Vantaa (Finavia)** operates a designated quiet-room service and, since the mid-2010s, has pushed passenger information to the Finavia app and away from routine PA [Source: https://www.finavia.fi/en/airports/helsinki-airport/airport/services-facilities/quiet-room]. Public reporting does not disclose missed-connection or gate-change performance data comparable with US hub peers. The operator should not import the model without empirical evidence of its behavior under IROPS at a slot-constrained US perimeter airport (DCA) or an international transfer hub with a fifteen-minute mean curb-to-gate journey (IAD).

### MWAA operating baseline

- **2025 combined system traffic** just under 54M passengers; IAD ~29M (10.5M international, both all-time highs); DCA ~24.9M (down 5.3 percent year over year, absorbing the January 2025 midair, the seven-week fall federal shutdown, and softer federal demand) [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md].
- **2025 Use & Lease:** 15-year residual, $9.0B Capital Construction Program ($6.99B IAD, $2.39B DCA), 140 percent coverage target [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md].
- **Concourse E:** 435,000 sq ft, 14 United gates, AeroTrain-connected, opening late 2026 [Source: https://thepointsguy.com/news/united-dream-terminal-dulles/].
- **July 2026 Dulles transformation announcement:** $20B–$22.5B multi-decade program to replace Concourses C and D, retire the mobile lounges, extend AeroTrain [Source: outputs/context/airport-context.md].

### IROPS events framing the operator's read

- **January 29, 2025** midair collision between PSA Airlines CRJ700 Flight 5342 and a U.S. Army UH-60 Black Hawk near DCA; 67 fatalities [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/Pages/DCA25MA108.aspx]. NTSB final findings January 2026 cited "deep, underlying systemic failures" including helicopter route design, an instrument failure that put the Black Hawk 100 feet off its perceived altitude, and tower communication and staffing problems [Source: https://www.npr.org/2026/01/27/nx-s1-5689091/ntsb-dca-midair-collision-black-hawk-helicopter].
- **July 3–4, 2026** DCA airspace closure (roughly noon to 1900 on July 4, extended disruption July 3) for the "Salute to America 250" National Special Security Event — an operating day removed from a slot-constrained field [Source: https://www.fodors.com/world/north-america/usa/washington-dc/experiences/news/washingtons-reagan-national-airport-will-temporarily-close-for-july-4-celebrations].
- Recurring convective ground stops routinely close DCA, IAD, and BWI simultaneously, pushing all three fields' hold rooms into IROPS mode within minutes and multiplying passenger reliance on airport-side information channels.

