## 3. The operational case FOR and AGAINST the thesis

### For (as I see it from the ops chair)

**One. It puts the operator in the design review.** Right now, acoustic performance is one line item on a consultant's spec, tuned by a subcontractor to hit an NFPA emergency threshold and not tuned at all for daily operations. The commissioning agent measures at CO, passes, and demobilizes. Two years later I am writing gate-change scripts that avoid Gates 14 and 15 because the announcement bleeds into Gate 12's boarding call and my gate agents have given up. A Design Manual standard with mandatory performance criteria — NC/RC by space type, T60 by geometry, STI in both emergency and daily modes — gives me a lever at the design-review table rather than a work-around post-CO.

**Two. It disciplines the visual and digital channel.** The reason the international "quiet" airports work at all is that FIDS content, app push, and screen density are ruthless — not that PA is absent. The current MWAA Design Manual, per the public record, does not specify FIDS pixel density per hold-room passenger, latency from gate change to FIDS refresh, or an app-notification SLA between airline systems and airport systems. Those specs are the operational teeth of any quiet standard. Without them the standard is a silence directive with no substitute channel behind it.

**Three. It gets ahead of Concourse E and the C/D replacement.** Concourse E opens late 2026 and will set the acoustic and PA template — good or bad — for the next generation of IAD midfield concourses. If we specify to Concourse E's package rather than adopt a general standard now, we lock in whatever the design team defaulted to, and every future concourse inherits it. The C/D replacement program will not accept a mid-design standard change without a claim.

**Four. It reduces employee exposure liability.** Hold rooms during peak IROPS routinely exceed 85 dBA in ways nobody measures because nobody in ops has been asked to. That is a Section 1910.95 exposure the moment we measure and record it. The workers' comp and hearing-conservation program cost is real and does not appear in any capital ROI model for terminal work — but it will appear on my P&L.

### Against (or: what will kill this in execution)

**One. A single prescriptive number applied to two different airports fails somewhere.** DCA T1 (1941, hard-plaster surfaces, ceiling that is aesthetically load-bearing and preservation-constrained) cannot hit the same reverberation target as IAD Concourse E (high volumes, curtain-wall glass). If the standard is written as one number, T1 either takes an exemption in month one — undermining the standard — or gets an expensive retrofit that was never scoped.

**Two. The airline broadcast-rights problem is not solved by an acoustic standard.** Under the 2025 Use & Lease, airlines have contractual boarding-call rights inside their leased envelope. The single loudest thing in an IAD hold room during IROPS is the gate-agent's handheld on speaker, not the airport PA. Any quiet standard that does not name and negotiate airline-side broadcast, tenant TVs, and third-party ground-handler communications is decorative.

**Three. IROPS is where the standard dies if it is not built for it.** On a bad-weather day at IAD, with rolling ground stops across the Baltimore-Washington triangle and hold rooms overflowing, the airport PA is the only channel that reaches every passenger in real time. A standard that pushes toward "no routine PA" without a fully specified IROPS-mode PA envelope — categories, scripts, zones, volumes, activation trigger — will fail on its first bad weekend. Every operator on the floor will just override it. And a standard everyone overrides is worse than no standard.

**Four. Commissioning is where standards die quietly.** Current commissioning practice is not sufficient to catch adjacent-zone spillover, PBB-alarm coupling into boarding-call intelligibility, or FIDS latency between the airline SOC feed and the airport display. If the standard is not paired with an acoustic-and-information-systems commissioning protocol funded as a project line item — not as a punch-list bolt-on — the standard is aspirational and the first post-opening ops audit will show it.

**Five. Board and airline optics in the wake of the January 2025 midair are unforgiving.** Any perceived "less notification" posture at DCA will be politically fraught. The framing must be information integrity and life-safety improvement, not "quieter." Called what it is, the standard is defensible. Called "quiet," it is a headline waiting to happen.

