## 4. Specific examples that frame the thesis concretely

**Example 1 — the IAD B-Concourse gate-change cascade during a de-ice event.** On a heavy de-ice morning at IAD, a single United mainline swap driven by a fielded PBB can cascade six adjacent gate reassignments across Concourse B in under forty minutes. PA is the only channel that reaches the eighteen-hundred-passenger hold-room load in real time; FIDS lags 60–120 seconds behind actual gate assignment as ramp control and the airline SOC negotiate. A no-routine-announcements standard applied naively to Concourse B in the C/D-replacement design would cost tens of missed connections per event and directly hit the slot-cost math for United's international bank. The right standard specifies an IROPS PA mode with different volume and frequency envelopes than daily mode, and ties the activation to the ops-center's IROPS trigger — not to a gate-agent's discretion.

**Example 2 — the DCA T1 acoustic envelope on a slot-constrained day.** T1 is 1941 Art Deco, hard-plaster surfaces, exposed structure, ceiling that is preservation-load-bearing. Its RT60 is what it is. Prescriptive reverberation language written for Concourse E's geometry will fail here or trigger a preservation-hostile retrofit. The right standard sets a mandatory speech-intelligibility floor at the NFPA envelope (STI ≥ 0.50 average, ≥ 0.45 in every ADS), but leaves the RT60 and NC targets as tiered and context-dependent with the equivalent STI outcome required. That is a standard the FAA, the AHJ, and the preservationists can all sign — one number-in-the-outcome, three paths to get there.

**Example 3 — the PBB-alarm coupling problem at a generic C/D-era gate.** Every operator has this gate. A PBB alarm sits at roughly 90 dBA at the gate desk. When the gate-agent's boarding call rides over it, actual intelligibility at row 10 of the hold room drops below STI 0.40 — worse than the emergency-notification floor NFPA requires. The airport never specified the PBB alarm level against boarding-call acoustics; the PBB manufacturer met their own spec. This is a Design Manual standard problem, solvable at roughly $8,000 per gate at design and roughly $80,000 per gate at retrofit. Concourse E has 14 gates. The C/D replacement will have many more. If the standard is not in the manual before Concourse E's PBB scope is set, MWAA pays retrofit prices at Concourse E and design prices at every subsequent concourse — with the acoustic bleed already baked into the first year of operating complaints.

**Example 4 — the fall 2025 seven-week federal shutdown at DCA.** During the shutdown, DCA hold-room staffing thinned as concessions rolled back hours and airline gate-agent staffing degraded. Passenger reliance on airport-side information — PA and FIDS — went up, not down, because the human information channel got sparser. A quiet standard that does not name a degraded-staffing contingency mode will meet its first real-world test badly and be judged on that test rather than on the ninety percent of days when it would perform well.

**Example 5 — the January 29, 2025 midair and NTSB posture.** The NTSB January 2026 findings on DCA25MA108 cited "deep, underlying systemic failures" including tower staffing and communication problems [Source: https://www.npr.org/2026/01/27/nx-s1-5689091/ntsb-dca-midair-collision-black-hawk-helicopter]. In that political environment, any MWAA action that reads as reducing communication redundancy at DCA — even if the substance is intelligibility-improving — will draw scrutiny it does not need. The standard must lead on life-safety, employee-exposure compliance, and IROPS resilience. "Quiet" is a communications problem, not a policy problem, but at DCA in 2026–2027 the communications problem is decisive.

---

## 5. Where I land

Adopt the standard. Put it in the MWAA Airports Authority Design Manual (IAD Vol. 1 airfield/signing, IAD Vol. 2 Main Terminal/Concourse Z, IAD Vol. 3 Concourse B, and the DCA parallel volumes) inside the FY26 revision cycle — before Concourse E's design-successor packages break ground, and before the DCA gate, curb, and terminal capital tranche locks its PA and FIDS scope. Structure it as **information integrity under stress,** not as "quiet."

- **Mandatory (both airports, all substantially renovated terminal space):** NFPA-derived STI floors verified by IEC 60268-16 STIPA at commissioning; adjacent-zone spillover limits; FIDS latency SLA between airline SOC feed and airport display; a fully specified IROPS PA mode explicitly separate from daily mode; PBB-alarm acoustic coordination with the boarding-call envelope; and named accessibility redundancies for each announcement category.
- **Recommended (verified by ops sign-off):** NC/RC targets by space type, quiet-zone locations, background-music policy, employee-exposure sound monitoring in identified hold rooms.
- **Context-dependent (documented exception with equivalent STI outcome required):** T60 targets in preservation-constrained envelopes (DCA T1 first among them), tenant television audio (existing leases run through 2039), and airline-side handheld amplification (subject to MII consultation).

Give the operator design-review sign-off authority on any deviation. Fund commissioning as a capital line item, not as a punch-list bolt-on. That is the difference between a standard that survives its first bad weather day and one that ends up in a binder on my shelf.

The strategist should take the thesis; the operator will make it work. But only if the standard is written for the Monday-morning push at 5:45 AM and the Friday-night thunderstorm at IAD, not for the atrium photograph on the annual report.

*— COO, MWAA (chair perspective)*
